  "slug": "inspection-before-dispatch-why-it-matters",
  "title": "Inspection Before Dispatch: Why Inspection Before Dispatch Matters",
  "seoTitle": "Inspection Before Dispatch: Why Inspection Before Dispatch Matters",
  "metaDescription": "Why construction plant should be inspected before it reaches your site, what a good check covers, and the questions to ask your supplier.",
  "primaryQuery": "equipment inspection before rental dispatch",
  "date": "2026-10-31",
  "read": "4 min",
  "img": "/img-14100101.jpg",
  "excerpt": "Why construction plant should be inspected before it reaches your site, what a good check covers, and the questions to ask your supplier.",
  "lead": "A machine checked before dispatch before dispatch is far more likely to work when it reaches your site. Problems like leaking hoses, weak batteries, worn parts or faulty safety devices are cheap to fix before dispatch and expensive to discover on site, where your crew, concrete and schedule are waiting. Always ask your rental supplier who checks the machine, what they check, and whether they will hand it over on site.",
  "body": "At RK Enterprises, \"dispatch-checked\" is not a slogan. It is the reason we exist. We have been on construction sites since we were fourteen, and we have watched too many mornings lost to a machine that should never have been dispatched.\n\n## The real cost of a machine that fails on arrival\n\nWhen hired plant arrives and does not work, the cost is not just the machine. It is:\n\n- **The crew standing idle**, being paid.\n- **Materials waiting** — sometimes concrete that cannot wait.\n- **The next trade delayed**, because your work did not finish.\n- **The client's trust**, which is hard to win back.\n\nA problem that takes twenty minutes to fix before dispatch can cost a full day on site.\n\n## Why before dispatch is the right time to check\n\nBefore dispatch there are tools, spares, light, space and people who know the machine. On a site there may be none of those — especially at 5 a.m., in the rain, in a tight Mumbai lane.\n\nChecking before dispatch also means a problem can be solved by sending a different machine, instead of sending the same one back.\n\n## What a good pre-dispatch check covers\n\nEvery class of machine is different, but a sensible check looks at:\n\n- **Fluids** — engine oil, hydraulic oil, coolant, fuel.\n- **Hoses and connections** — leaks, cracks, loose fittings.\n- **Electrics** — battery, lights, horn, warning devices.\n- **Controls** — every function moving as it should.\n- **Safety devices** — limits, alarms, emergency stops, guards.\n- **Tyres or tracks** — wear, damage, pressure.\n- **Attachments and accessories** — present and in working order.\n- **A short run** — the machine actually working, not just starting.\n\n## Questions to ask any rental supplier\n\nWhether you hire from us or anyone else:\n\n1. Is every machine checked before it is dispatched?\n2. Who does the check — someone who knows this machine?\n3. Will someone hand it over and brief my crew on site?\n4. If it fails anyway, will you repair on site or swap it?\n5. What time does your phone get answered?\n\nClear answers mean a supplier who takes it seriously.\n\n## What to check when it arrives\n\nThe pre-dispatch check is the first line. Your own quick check on arrival is the second:\n\n- Walk around the machine — any visible leaks or damage?\n- Start it and run the main functions.\n- Check safety devices and lights.\n- Confirm attachments are the ones you asked for.\n- Note anything odd and tell the supplier immediately.\n\nTake photos at handover. They protect both sides.\n\n## Why it matters even more in Mumbai\n\nMumbai sites are tight. Access windows can be short. Some deliveries only happen at certain hours. If a machine fails at the gate, there may be no space to work on it and no second chance that day. During monsoon, wet conditions add more risk to electrics and ground stability.\n\nThat is why we check machines before they leave — so the problem, if there is one, stays with us and not on your site.\n\n## Pre-dispatch checks and breakdown support go together\n\nNo check makes a machine perfect forever. Machines are machines. What a pre-dispatch check does is remove the avoidable failures. For the rest, you need a supplier who responds: repair on site, or a replacement so the schedule keeps moving.\n\n## How RK Enterprises does it\n\n- Every machine inspected before dispatch.\n- Delivery planned around your site's access.\n- On-site handover and briefing by people who know the machine.\n- We stay until the job is moving.\n- Phone answered from 5 a.m.\n- Breakdown response: repair on site or swap.\n\nFor buyers, [RSP Sale](/services/rsp-sale) includes an inspection report before handover.\n\n\n\n**Want plant that works when it arrives?** Call or WhatsApp RK Enterprises with your site, dates and task. [See RSP Rental →](/services/rsp-rental)",
